          ":question/id": "7e1b8c44-a657-5553-a4bd-2f5094dfbeda",
          ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{26}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 26 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}\u206126 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{26}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}\u206126}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{26}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}\u206126}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}\u206126}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061a-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u206126)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u206126) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
